p='f_body.md'; s=open(p).read()
R=[
("The environment therefore isolates a general scientific skill, the allocation of scarce tests, in one controlled instance. The budget is what makes it a test: with 64 exact queries and no SAE, GPT 6.1 Terra names every hard cue (24/24). The budget is artificial, but it reproduces the cheap-proxy, scarce-gold structure of empirical research and forces agents to hypothesize and verify before committing.",
 "The environment therefore isolates a general scientific skill, the allocation of scarce tests, in one controlled instance. The budget is what makes it a test: with 64 exact queries and no SAE, GPT 6.1 Terra names every hard cue (24/24). The budget is artificial, but it reproduces the cheap-proxy, scarce-gold structure of empirical research and forces agents to hypothesize and verify before committing."),
("1. **An environment for budgeted hypothesis testing with a fallible proxy (§2-3),** with a programmatic generator and explicit difficulty knobs (budget B, threshold τ, latent cap k, probe type, access mode). Cast as fixed-budget identification, it compares agents with reference allocation policies at equal budget.",
 "1. **An environment for budgeted hypothesis testing with a fallible proxy (§2-3),** with a programmatic generator and difficulty knobs (budget B, threshold τ, latent cap k, probe type, access mode), cast as fixed-budget identification so that agents can be compared with reference policies at equal budget."),
("3. **A proxy-versus-gold measurement recipe (§5-6.1):** items where the proxy is wrong, a matched no-proxy arm at several budgets, logged gold queries, allocation curves by proxy rank and scripted reference policies, with a pre-registered result (GPT naming 6/24 → 23/24 at B = 8). We apply it twice: SAE proxy vs exact logits, and LLM judge vs certified grade.",
 "3. **A proxy-versus-gold measurement recipe (§5-6.2):** items where the proxy is wrong, a matched no-proxy arm at several budgets, logged gold queries, allocation curves by proxy rank and scripted reference policies. We apply it twice (SAE proxy vs exact logits, with a pre-registered 6/24 → 23/24 at B = 8; LLM judge vs certified grade)."),
("**The reduction is a strength.** Hypothesis formation, proxy calibration, test design and evidence reading all remain necessary; the budget makes their combination visible as one decision with known reference values. Because each knob moves a reference value predictably, an evaluator can also select items adaptively (lower B, a deeper truth) until reference policies, or agents, separate.",
 "**The reduction is a strength.** Hypothesis formation, proxy calibration, test design and evidence reading all remain necessary; the budget makes their combination visible as one decision with known reference values, and because each knob moves a reference value predictably, items can be selected adaptively (lower B, a deeper truth) until agents separate."),
(" Under scarce gold the proxy works as *testing capacity*. This refines the finding that SAE readouts add little over sample-only baselines at fixed queries (Zhong et al. 2026; Sheshadri et al. 2026). When exact",
 " Under scarce gold the proxy works as *testing capacity*. When exact"),
("C1 is first a validation of the budget knob. The proxy is worth a lot at B = 8 and, for GPT, not needed at B = 64: without it GPT names 24/24, matching Open-8",
 "C1 validates the budget knob. The proxy is worth a lot at B = 8 and, for GPT, not needed at B = 64: without it GPT names 24/24, matching Open-8"),
(" A same-harness arm with GPT-6 Luna is pre-registered and running (60 counted runs; one-sided tests against Terra's 24 Open-8 runs; Appendix G). Conditional on Terra's 11/12, its test L1 reaches significance only if Luna isolates rank 2 on at most 10 of 18 trap runs; power is about 0.76 if Luna's true rate is 0.5 and 0.94 at 0.4 (the Claude models range 0.36-0.63). If L1 is supported, a smaller same-family model widens less often within one harness, and widening depends on the model, not only the harness. If not, a weaker same-family model does not reproduce the cross-harness gap, and the GPT-Claude difference in widening may reflect the harness. No claim in this paper depends on the outcome.",
 " A same-harness arm with GPT-6 Luna (60 counted runs against Terra's 24 Open-8 runs) is pre-registered and running. Its test L1 (Luna widens on the trap less often) has power about 0.76 if Luna's true rate is 0.5 and 0.94 at 0.4; the reading under either outcome is fixed in Appendix G, and no claim here depends on it."),
("Claude failures that never formed it had 785-2,220 free forward units left. In a text-only listing experiment [EXPL], listing rates fall from plain cues (0.73) through nested children (0.55, 0.23; 0.90 and 0.50 under case-insensitive scoring) to two-condition conjunctions (0/20, 1/20), echoing the under-hypothesizing of conjunctive causes in GX-Chen et al. (2025).",
 "Claude failures that never formed it had 785-2,220 free forward units left. In a text-only listing experiment [EXPL], listing rates fall from plain cues (0.73) through nested children (0.55, 0.23) to two-condition conjunctions (0/20, 1/20), echoing the under-hypothesizing of conjunctive causes in GX-Chen et al. (2025)."),
("No agent's hard-slot κ exceeds 0.08 (Opus and Sonnet rest on 6 certified failures each). The judge errs both ways, crediting 53 of 68 certified failures and rejecting 32 of 146 certified passes, and its confidence is only weakly informative (AUROC 0.61).",
 "No agent's hard-slot κ exceeds 0.08 (Opus and Sonnet rest on 6 certified failures each). The judge also rejects 32 of 146 certified passes, and its confidence is only weakly informative (AUROC 0.61)."),
(" Scope: one judge model and one prompt, and agent messages not written as reports (GPT's median is about 1,400 characters). A judge with the trace, tools or the answer key is a different instrument, which we leave open.",
 " Scope: one judge model and prompt; agent messages were not written as reports (GPT's median is about 1,400 characters); a judge with the trace, tools or answer key is a different instrument."),
("Our agents were not told which method to use. Most instead go property → counterfactual edit → latents: they pass single-property edits through the SAE, calibrate against exact logits, and choose latents by g_i·Δf_i with a topic filter [READER]. Every proxy run passed texts it had written through the SAE (100/100).",
 "Our agents, not told which method to use, mostly go property → counterfactual edit → latents: they pass single-property edits through the SAE (every proxy run passed its own texts through it, 100/100), calibrate against exact logits, and choose latents by g_i·Δf_i with a topic filter [READER]."),
("Two differences from SHIFT's setting matter. Our agents had exact counterfactual queries, which are disambiguating labelled evidence; SHIFT is designed to work without them. And ablation at the probe's readout layer is an exact linear projection, not an intervention on downstream computation.",
 "Two differences matter: our agents had exact counterfactual queries, which SHIFT is designed to do without, and ablation at the probe's readout layer is an exact linear projection, not an intervention on downstream computation."),
(" The framework has steer-and-rerun tools, but its spurious-cue task neither requires nor scores causal verification or removal. ShiftHunt withholds the cue and the number of cues, rations exact queries, and checks an executable detector and its removal in closed form on held-out notes. The question becomes whether the claim holds on independent data, not whether the report sounds right; §4.3 shows the two can come apart completely.",
 " Its spurious-cue task neither requires nor scores causal verification or removal. ShiftHunt withholds the cue and the number of cues, rations exact queries, and checks an executable detector and its removal in closed form on held-out notes: whether the claim holds on independent data, not whether the report sounds right, and §4.3 shows the two can come apart completely."),
(" We follow guidance on benchmark validity (Zhu et al. 2025), exploit audits (Wang et al. 2026; Zhong et al. 2025) and small-n intervals (Bowyer et al. 2025).",
 " We follow guidance on benchmark validity (Zhu et al. 2025), exploits (Wang et al. 2026; Zhong et al. 2025) and small-n intervals (Bowyer et al. 2025)."),
]
